course: dictionary
lesson: 3722
title: Meaning from the action
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Some words show a feeling by describing a movement. Flinch is one of them. When you flinch, you make a small, quick movement backwards or away, because something hurts, frightens or surprises you. The word tells you what the body does, so you can picture it.
explain: «Getting hurt in front of people adds embarrassment to pain; flinching when a ball comes near is normal for a while afterwards.» The word flinching is linked to a ball coming near. The sentence says this is normal, so the reader knows the movement is a natural reaction to something that might hurt.
explain: Flinch can also be used with «from». To flinch from something means to move away from it because you are scared. But if someone does not flinch, they stay still and brave. Look at the words around the verb to find out whether the meaning is fear or courage.
fact: To flinch means to make a small, quick movement backwards or away because of pain, fear or surprise.
fact: «Flinching» is the -ing form, and «flinched» is the past tense: she flinched when the door slammed.
fact: «Without flinching» means without moving away, so the person stays calm and brave.
fact: «Didn't flinch» means did not move back, even though something might have been frightening or painful.
fact: A flinch can also be a noun: a flinch is one small, quick movement away.
exercise: «The goalkeeper did not flinch as the ball hurtled towards him.» What does did not flinch mean? | Kicked the ball back hard | * Stayed still and brave, without moving away | Ran off the pitch in fright | Cried loudly at the crowd
exercise: «Ben flinched when the dog barked suddenly.» What did Ben do? | * Made a small, quick movement away | Fell asleep on the grass | Laughed and clapped | Barked back at the dog
exercise: Find and copy the word that means a small, quick movement away. | sudden | ball | goalkeeper | * flinch
test: «Lily flinched at the loud bang.» What does flinched mean? | Ran into the room | Fell asleep | Smiled widely | * Made a small, quick movement away
test: Mum says, «Don't flinch from your homework, just start.» What does flinch from mean here? | * Avoid it because it feels scary | Hide it under the bed | Forget it completely | Face it bravely without avoiding it
exam: Which sentence uses flinch as a noun? | * The needle made me give a small flinch | They flinched backwards | I flinch when the bell rings | She will flinch tomorrow
